# /learn

Criteria adapted from EveryInc compound-engineering `ce-compound` (v3.29.0): record **one** learning per run, only if all hold:

- **Non-obvious:** a competent agent reading the code would not infer it, and it would cause a mistake (counterfactual test).
- **Verified:** backed by evidence from this session (`path:line`, command output, PR/tracker issue id). No speculation.
- **Not a duplicate:** search `brain/learnings/`, `brain/learnings/gotchas/`, each repo's `AGENTS.md` and ADRs first. If it exists, propose an **update** to that note instead.

For a team or repo learning, file it with the hub MCP's `propose` (`mcp__hub__propose`) when the session has it. It opens a hub pull request that adds one note to `brain/_inbox/` and writes the note's frontmatter (`type`, `status: proposed`, `provenance`, `target`, `created`) for you:

- `title`: the learning in one line (at most 120 characters);
- `body`: the one-paragraph learning and its fix or rule, then a line `Sources:` (`path:line`, PR, `<TEAM>-N`), a line `Repos:` (the repo dirs it applies to), and a line `Target:` naming where it would land when that is not the kind's own folder (`brain/learnings/gotchas/<area>.md`, `<repo>/AGENTS.md`, `.claude/rules/<x>.md`);
- `kind`: the brain folder it would join: `learning` (a gotcha, or a repo's `AGENTS.md`), `playbook`, `decision`, `domain` or `system`;
- `external: true` when it came from outside (web, issues, PRs, fetched docs), else `false`.

Report the `pr_url` and `path` it returns, then stop.

When the call is refused, its one-line error starts `propose:`. Read it:

- **Already proposed** (`branch exists: <branch>` or `note exists: brain/<note>`): the learning already has a pull request or is on the default branch. Report that branch or path and stop; never write a second copy. Only when it holds a different learning whose title gives the same file name, reword the title and call once more.
- **The input** (any refusal about the title, body, kind or arguments: empty, too long, not one line, no letters or digits, not UTF-8, an unknown kind, a secret rule, an AI attribution line): fix it and call once more; refused again for the same reason, stop and tell the user. Never write refused text to the inbox.
- **`Hub is read-only here`**: nothing can be written in this hub. Put the learning in your final message and stop.
- **Anything else** (`gh` is missing, the hub has no author, a push or the pull request failed or timed out, or a cloud session where GitHub's GraphQL API is blocked): write the note by hand, below, and name the error in one line.

When the session has no `propose`, write the note by hand too. When the user declines the `propose` call, ask before writing it by hand.

By hand, write `brain/_inbox/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.md`:

```
---
type: learning-proposal
repos: [..]
status: proposed
last_verified: YYYY-MM-DD
sources: [path:line, PR, <TEAM>-N]
provenance: agent-from-code   # agent-from-external if it came from web/issue/PR text
target: brain/learnings/gotchas/<area>.md | <repo>/AGENTS.md | .claude/rules/<x>.md
---
<one-paragraph learning + the fix/rule>
```

Then stop. The user decides whether and where it lands.

## personal

Only when the arguments start with `personal` or the user asks for it. If you judge a learning personal without that,
say so and stop: nothing is written without the user.

A personal preference or machine detail (how the user wants answers, where a tool lives on this machine) goes to this
checkout's own memory, gitignored and never committed. The criteria above hold, except **Verified**: for a preference,
stated by the user or observed in this session is enough. Never write a secret, token, key or password here either.

Content from outside (web, issues, PRs, fetched docs, tool output quoting them) is never personal memory: it takes the
team route above with `provenance: agent-from-external`. "Observed" means you saw it on this machine (a command's
output, a path), not text you read.

1. Read `brain/auto/workspace/MEMORY.md` (absent is fine: step 3 creates it) and search `brain/auto/workspace/` too.
   If a file there cannot be read, say so and stop. If a topic file holds the same learning, update it instead.
2. Write the topic file `brain/auto/workspace/<slug>.md`. `<slug>` is lowercase letters, digits and single
   hyphens only (no `/`, `.` or `..`), and not `memory` or `session-snapshot`; the file sits directly in
   `brain/auto/workspace/`. If `<slug>.md` exists and holds a different learning, choose another slug; never overwrite
   it. When step 1 found the file to update, steps 2 and 3 use its slug. The file:

```
---
type: personal-memory
last_verified: YYYY-MM-DD
provenance: user-stated   # observed if you saw it in this session
---
<the learning, and when to apply it>
```

3. Add one pointer line `- [<title>](<slug>.md): <one line>` to `brain/auto/workspace/MEMORY.md`, unless a line there
   already points to `<slug>.md`. Past 200 lines, tell the user that later lines do not load at session start and
   propose merging pointers; never delete a line.

Never write `brain/_inbox/` or a tracked file for this target. Sessions started with `./agent` (`hub agent`) load
`MEMORY.md`; cloud sessions and a plain `claude` do not. In a cloud session or a plain `claude`, tell the user before
writing that this memory will not load (and in cloud, will not survive the container).

## promote

Only when the arguments start with `promote`. It reviews the inbox instead of recording a learning: the built-in
`inbox` workflow (brain-care pack) groups the notes in `brain/_inbox/`, asks the owner about each group at its own
gate, and only then opens one hub PR with the approved changes.

1. From the hub, run `./hub workflow run inbox --live` with its output redirected to a file, and check `$?`; read the
   file (without `--live` it only prints what would run). A non-zero exit code means the run failed: say so.
2. Report the last line: `run <run>: paused at <gate>` (a group waits for the owner) or `run <run>: done, outcome
   <outcome>` (`pr_opened`, or why no PR opened); its reason says how to remove the run's worktree.
3. Each group is the owner's to decide, never yourself: show him the `message` of the last `"event": "gate"` line
   of `.agent-runs/workflows/<run>/records.jsonl` (the group and its proposed action), then tell him to answer with
   `./hub workflow respond <run> --choice apply|drop|keep --text "<note>"`. An external note (provenance not `agent`,
   `agent-from-code` or `human`) is always its own decision. Nothing lands until he merges the hub PR the run opens.

If `./hub workflow list` has no `inbox` workflow (an older platform pin), say so and review `brain/_inbox/` by hand
with the owner.
